.TH DAQ-OPERATIONS.YAML 5 "2026-10-06" "mu2edaq-shifter-tools 1.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
daq-operations.yaml \- DAQ partitions, environments and ports for the shifter tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I config/daq\-operations.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
The operations file describes the DAQ partitions that
.BR start-daq.sh (1),
.BR stop-daq.sh (1),
.BR daq-status.sh (1),
.BR setup-online.sh (1),
.BR create-environment.sh (1)
and
.BR send-run-control-command.sh (1)
manage. No shell command parses it; each reads one value at a time
through
.BR daq-read-config (1).
.PP
The shipped \fIconfig/daq\-operations.yaml\fR is a tracked symbolic
link to \fIdaq\-operations.example.yaml\fR, so a fresh checkout runs.
.SS Top level
.TP
.B partitions
Mapping of partition name to partition (required).
.TP
.B resource_manager_port
Integer, default 1973. Returned by
.BR "daq-read-config resource-manager-port" ;
nothing in the package uses it.
.SS Partition
.TP
.B environments
Mapping of environment name to environment.
.TP
.B active_environments
List (or single string) of the environments
.BR start-daq.sh (1)
starts and
.BR stop-daq.sh (1)
stops. When absent, every environment is active.
.TP
.B base_release
Path of the base release, the spack upstream for
.BR create-environment.sh (1).
.TP
.B ots_port_offset
Integer, default 0.
.SS Environment
.TP
.B test_rel_path
Test release directory; \fBsetup-online.sh\fR changes into it.
.TP
.B setup_cmd
Command sourced in that directory, arguments included, e.g.
\fI<test_rel_path>/setup_ots.sh tracker\fR. The argument is passed to
\fBsetup_ots.sh\fR as its subsystem name.
.TP
.B port_offset
Integer, default 0. \fBots_port_offset\fR + \fBport_offset\fR is the
\fBgateway\-port\fR verb.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/daq\-operations.example.yaml
Shipped example; also the marker used to find the installation root.
.PP
The file is looked up on the search path \fB$MU2EDAQ_CONFIG_DIR\fR,
\fI./config\fR, \fI$MU2EDAQ_ROOT/config\fR, \fI~/.config/mu2edaq\fR,
\fI/etc/mu2edaq\fR, unless \fB\-c\fR or \fBMU2EDAQ_CONFIG\fR names it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_CONFIG
Explicit path to the file.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_CONFIG_DIR
First directory on the search path.
.SH EXAMPLES
The shipped example, abbreviated:
.PP
.RS
.nf
partitions:
  partition_0:
    environments:
      tracker:
        test_rel_path: "/home/mu2edaq/tracker/test_rel_v9_00_00"
        setup_cmd: "/home/mu2edaq/tracker/test_rel_v9_00_00/setup_ots.sh tracker"
        port_offset: 10
    base_release: "/mu2e/releases/v9_00_00"
    active_environments: ["tracker", "trigger"]
    ots_port_offset: 10000
resource_manager_port: 1973
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
The example's \fBcalorimeter\fR environment passes \fBcalorimeter\fR to
\fBsetup_ots.sh\fR. Every \fBsetup_ots.sh\fR copy in otsdaq\-mu2e\-config
and mu2edaq\-operations accepts only \fBcalo\fR and answers
\fBInvalid parameter!\fR to anything else. The environment is commented
out of \fBactive_environments\fR in the example.
.PP
The example's gateway ports (10010 tracker, 10020 trigger) are not the
\fBOTS_MAIN_PORT\fR values those \fBsetup_ots.sh\fR copies assign (3065
for both).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR daq-read-config (1),
